<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Celenderis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Celenderis was an ancient port city on the coast of Rough <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, at modern Aydıncık in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mersin">Mersin</a> Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Greek tradition credited colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> with its foundation, and it later belonged to the Persian, Hellenistic and Roman worlds as a harbour for crossings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Remains include city walls, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and Roman baths.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/celenderis.jpg" alt="Celenderis" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AydincikBucht.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
